// Practice quiz: Debugging with logs and exec
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: containers-cicd/containers-and-compose/debugging-with-logs-and-exec

::o1 logs of one service::Which command shows the output of only the worker service in a Compose stack? {
=docker compose logs worker #Correct. Name the service to see its output alone.
~docker compose logs #Not quite. That interleaves every service.
~docker compose exec worker logs #Not quite. exec runs a command inside the container; logs is a compose subcommand.
~docker compose ps worker #Not quite. ps shows state, not output.
}

::o1 follow::What does adding -f to docker compose logs api do? {
=Keeps printing new lines as api writes them, until you press Ctrl+C #Correct. -f follows the log.
~Forces the container to restart #Not quite. It only reads the output.
~Shows only failed lines #Not quite. It shows all lines and keeps following.
~Saves the logs to a file #Not quite. It prints to your terminal.
}

::o2 exec a command::Which command prints the value of REDIS_HOST inside the running cache service container? {
=docker compose exec cache printenv REDIS_HOST #Correct. exec runs a command inside the running service container.
~docker compose logs cache REDIS_HOST #Not quite. logs prints output; it runs no command.
~docker compose exec printenv REDIS_HOST #Not quite. The service name comes before the command.
~docker compose run REDIS_HOST cache #Not quite. That is not how you print a variable inside a running container.
}

::o2 exec needs running::You run docker compose exec api printenv and get service "api" is not running. Why? {
=exec only works in a running container, and api has stopped #Correct. Read its logs to find out why it stopped.
~printenv is not installed in any image #Not quite. The message says the service is not running.
~exec needs -d #Not quite. -d does not make a stopped container run.
~The service name must be in capitals #Not quite. Service names are written as in compose.yaml.
}

::o3 exited container::docker compose ps -a shows api as Exited (1). What is the best first check? {
=docker compose logs api #Correct. The reason it exited is usually in its last lines.
~docker compose exec api sh #Not quite. You cannot exec into a stopped container.
~docker compose down -v #Not quite. That deletes volumes and tells you nothing.
~Rebuild every image #Not quite. Find the cause first.
}

::o3 unreachable::Your browser cannot open http\://localhost\:8080. What is the best first check? {
=docker compose ps, to see whether the service runs and which port it publishes #Correct. State and published ports come first.
~docker compose exec api printenv #Not quite. Check that it runs and is published first.
~docker compose logs db #Not quite. The database is not what the browser calls.
~Restart your laptop #Not quite. Check the stack's state first.
}
